¶ 1. *Compass of the Human Voice.*

Instruments can be tuned or manufactured at almost any required pitch. The human voice is born, not manufactured. Although by skilful training its compass @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
can generally be somewhat extended, both upwards and downwards, yet it must in general be considered to be an instrument beyond human control. The usages of Europe have, however, made it the principal instrument, and, when it is present, have reduced all others to an @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
accompaniment. Hence it is necessary that these other instruments should have their compass and pitch regulated by that of the human voice. Now the voice, like the viol family, represents at least four different instruments—soprano, alto, tenor, and bass, with two @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
intermediate ones, ¶ mezzo-soprano, between soprano and alto, and barytone, between tenor and bass. It is therefore as necessary to determine the average and exceptional compass of these species of voice as it is to know the compass of any other instrument, in order @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
that composers may be certain as to what sounds can be reproduced, and not demand any other. To do this, the precise acoustic meaning of each written musical note should be ascertained. The difficulty of determining it has been shewn by the preceding history of musical @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
pitch (pp. 494-513), from which, combined with the tables of mean-tone and equal intonation (pp. 434 and 437), it is evident that Handel's sustained *a''* in the *Hallelujah* chorus had 845 vib., but would now be sung to 904 vib.; and that Mozart's *f'''* in the @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
*Zauberflöte* would have meant 1349 ¶ vib., but would now have to be sung at 1455 vib. The strain that this would put upon voices is evident, and no composer who wished his music to be well represented would think of making such demands on his singers. It appeared, @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
therefore, necessary to ascertain more precisely than had been hitherto done, and to express in numbers of vibrations, the limits of the different kinds of voices. If the composer will then only translate his written notes into numbers of vibrations, by the table on p. @@R1@@
>>>
<<<
@@R1@@
===
437, according to the pitch he employs, he will avoid all danger of straining singers.

Through the kindness and liberality of the choir conductors, Messrs. Henry Leslie, W. G. McNaught, J. Proudman, Ebenezer Prout, L. C. & G. J. Venables, and 542 members of the choirs @@R1@@
